insert into kairo.questions (id, subject, topic, subtopic, learning_objective, concepts_tested, difficulty_rating, cognitive_level, estimated_solving_time_sec, reading_load, calculation_load, distractors, source, year, exam_body, stem, options, correct_option, explanation, lifecycle_state, distractor_rationale) values
($q$chemistry_0541$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Equilibrium$q$, $q$Le Chatelier's Principle — Pressure & Temperature Effects$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Effect of temperature on exothermic equilibria.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kzcd9t", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This has the effect backwards - increasing temperature actually speeds up and favours the reverse reaction here, since it's the endothermic direction, not the one that gets slowed down.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Since this reaction has a nonzero ΔH, temperature changes definitely shift the equilibrium position - assuming 'no effect' skips over the exothermic/endothermic nature of the reaction entirely.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This is the reverse of what actually happens - since the forward reaction releases heat, adding more heat pushes the system away from the forward direction, not towards it.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$H3PO4(aq) + H2O(l) ↔ H2PO4-(aq) + H3O+(aq), ΔH = -13kJ at 298K. In the reaction above, an increase in temperature would$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "slow down the reverse reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "have no effect on the reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "favour the reverse reaction", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "favour the forward reaction", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$The negative ΔH tells you the forward reaction releases heat - it's exothermic. That automatically makes the reverse reaction endothermic (it absorbs heat). By Le Chatelier's principle, adding heat to a system pushes the equilibrium in whichever direction soaks up that extra heat, so raising the temperature here favours the reverse (endothermic) reaction.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This has the effect backwards - increasing temperature actually speeds up and favours the reverse reaction here, since it's the endothermic direction, not the one that gets slowed down. B: Since this reaction has a nonzero ΔH, temperature changes definitely shift the equilibrium position - assuming 'no effect' skips over the exothermic/endothermic nature of the reaction entirely. D: This is the reverse of what actually happens - since the forward reaction releases heat, adding more heat pushes the system away from the forward direction, not towards it.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0542$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Electrochemistry$q$, $q$Daniell cell reactions$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Daniell cell reactions.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "23bs2u", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Direct combination describes two elements joining to form a single compound, like sodium reacting with chlorine - it doesn't capture the separated half-reactions and electron transfer happening in a cell.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Double decomposition describes an exchange of ions between two compounds in solution, typically producing a precipitate - it has nothing to do with the electron transfer through a wire that defines an electrochemical cell.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Neutralization specifically refers to an acid reacting with a base to form salt and water - it's an entirely different category of reaction from the electron-transfer chemistry happening in a Daniell cell.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The reaction that takes place in Daniell's cell is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "direct combination", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "redox", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "double decomposition", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "neutralization", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$In a Daniell cell, zinc metal loses electrons (oxidation) at one electrode while copper ions gain electrons (reduction) at the other, with the electrons travelling through an external wire to generate current. That paired loss-and-gain of electrons is the definition of a redox reaction, and it's exactly what makes the whole cell work as a source of electricity.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Direct combination describes two elements joining to form a single compound, like sodium reacting with chlorine - it doesn't capture the separated half-reactions and electron transfer happening in a cell. C: Double decomposition describes an exchange of ions between two compounds in solution, typically producing a precipitate - it has nothing to do with the electron transfer through a wire that defines an electrochemical cell. D: Neutralization specifically refers to an acid reacting with a base to form salt and water - it's an entirely different category of reaction from the electron-transfer chemistry happening in a Daniell cell.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0543$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Reactivity and Electrochemical Series$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Displacement reactions and the electrochemical series.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "e6wz62", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "This has the electron flow backwards - zinc is the more reactive metal, so it's zinc that loses electrons (is oxidized), sending electrons towards the copper ions, not the other way round.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "The zinc rod actually does dissolve - it's being oxidized into Zn2+ ions that enter the solution as the reaction proceeds, which is exactly what drives the whole displacement.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This is the opposite of what happens - copper ions in solution are being consumed and reduced to solid copper metal, so their concentration decreases, not increases.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$What happens when a zinc rod is immersed in copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) solution?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "The blue colour of the solution disappears", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "Electrons flow from copper to zinc", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "The zinc rod remains undissolved", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "The copper generates more copper ions", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Zinc is more reactive than copper, so it displaces copper straight out of solution: Zn(s) + Cu2+(aq) → Zn2+(aq) + Cu(s). As the reaction proceeds, blue Cu2+ ions are steadily replaced by colourless Zn2+ ions, so the characteristic blue colour of the copper sulphate solution gradually fades away, while a coating of reddish-brown copper metal builds up on the zinc rod.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: This has the electron flow backwards - zinc is the more reactive metal, so it's zinc that loses electrons (is oxidized), sending electrons towards the copper ions, not the other way round. C: The zinc rod actually does dissolve - it's being oxidized into Zn2+ ions that enter the solution as the reaction proceeds, which is exactly what drives the whole displacement. D: This is the opposite of what happens - copper ions in solution are being consumed and reduced to solid copper metal, so their concentration decreases, not increases.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0544$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Energy Changes$q$, $q$Interpreting Enthalpy Sign and Magnitude$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Exothermic and endothermic reactions.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kshq1j", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Spontaneity is actually governed by Gibbs free energy (which factors in entropy too), not by ΔH alone - this reaction being exothermic doesn't, by itself, directly prove spontaneity in the way the question is testing.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This is the exact opposite of what a negative ΔH means - endothermic reactions absorb heat and have a positive ΔH, not a negative one.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "The single arrow shows this reaction going to completion in one direction, not an equilibrium (which would use a double-headed harpoon arrow) - and ΔH's sign doesn't tell you anything about equilibrium status anyway.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$C2H4(g) + H2(g) → C2H6(g), ΔH = -137 kJ mol-1. The reaction represented above is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "exothermic", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "spontaneous", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "endothermic", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "in equilibrium", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$The sign of ΔH tells you everything you need here: a negative value means the reaction releases heat into the surroundings as it happens, which is precisely the definition of exothermic.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Spontaneity is actually governed by Gibbs free energy (which factors in entropy too), not by ΔH alone - this reaction being exothermic doesn't, by itself, directly prove spontaneity in the way the question is testing. C: This is the exact opposite of what a negative ΔH means - endothermic reactions absorb heat and have a positive ΔH, not a negative one. D: The single arrow shows this reaction going to completion in one direction, not an equilibrium (which would use a double-headed harpoon arrow) - and ΔH's sign doesn't tell you anything about equilibrium status anyway.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0545$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemistry & Industry$q$, $q$null$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Industrial uses of tartaric acid.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "4yozg9", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Tartaric acid naturally occurs in grapes and other fruits, but that's different from being used industrially to 'make' fruit juices - confusing where a substance is found with what it's manufactured for.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Rust removal is more commonly associated with other organic acids, like oxalic acid - mixing up which specific acid does which specific industrial job is an easy trap.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Drying agents are typically desiccants like silica gel or anhydrous salts - that's not a property or use associated with tartaric acid at all.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Tartaric acid is used industrially to$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "make baking powder", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "make fruit juices", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "remove rust", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "dry substances", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Tartaric acid is a key ingredient in baking powder, where it reacts with sodium bicarbonate to release carbon dioxide gas - that's what makes baked goods rise. It's this specific industrial application, not its natural presence in food, that the question is testing.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Tartaric acid naturally occurs in grapes and other fruits, but that's different from being used industrially to 'make' fruit juices - confusing where a substance is found with what it's manufactured for. C: Rust removal is more commonly associated with other organic acids, like oxalic acid - mixing up which specific acid does which specific industrial job is an easy trap. D: Drying agents are typically desiccants like silica gel or anhydrous salts - that's not a property or use associated with tartaric acid at all.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0546$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Acids, Bases & Salts$q$, $q$Acidic and Basic Ions — Salt Hydrolysis$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Nature of salts from acid-base combinations.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "yzd6cv", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "'Complex' refers to coordination compounds built around ligands bonded to a central metal ion - it's an unrelated concept to a salt's acid-base character.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "A basic salt comes from a weak acid reacting with a strong base, like sodium ethanoate - not from a strong acid and strong base combination.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "An acidic salt comes from a strong acid reacting with a weak base, like ammonium chloride - that's a different pairing from the one described here.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The salt formed from a strong acid and a strong base is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "complex", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "neutral", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "basic", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "acidic", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$When a strong acid and a strong base react, both the resulting cation and anion are 'spectator' ions that don't hydrolyse in water - neither one pulls the solution's pH away from 7. Think of table salt, NaCl, formed from HCl and NaOH: it dissolves to give a perfectly neutral solution. That's the general rule for strong-acid-plus-strong-base salts.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: 'Complex' refers to coordination compounds built around ligands bonded to a central metal ion - it's an unrelated concept to a salt's acid-base character. C: A basic salt comes from a weak acid reacting with a strong base, like sodium ethanoate - not from a strong acid and strong base combination. D: An acidic salt comes from a strong acid reacting with a weak base, like ammonium chloride - that's a different pairing from the one described here.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0547$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Energy Changes$q$, $q$Entropy and Disorder$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Entropy changes on dissolution.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "s55vck", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "A negative ΔG is a general feature of any spontaneous process, but it isn't the specific property this question is highlighting - it's testing the entropy change from the ordered solid breaking apart into dispersed ions, not the overall free energy condition.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This has it backwards - dissolving breaks apart the ordered solid lattice into freely moving, dispersed ions, which is an increase in disorder, not a decrease.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Dissolving NaOH is actually exothermic - it releases heat and noticeably warms the solution, unlike substances such as ammonium nitrate that cool the solution down when they dissolve. Assuming all dissolution is cooling is the trap here.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Dissolving some pellets of NaOH in water would result in$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "a decrease in the free energy of the solution", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "a decrease in the entropy of the solution", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "a lowering of the temperature of the solution", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "an increase in the entropy of the solution", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Picture what's physically happening: solid NaOH starts out as a rigid, highly ordered ionic lattice, with every Na+ and OH- locked into a fixed position. Once it dissolves, those ions break free and disperse randomly throughout the water. That's a clear jump from an ordered state to a disordered one, which is exactly what an increase in entropy means. This is also a good demonstration reaction because it's noticeably exothermic - the container gets warm, not cold - so you can rule out any option that claims cooling.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: A negative ΔG is a general feature of any spontaneous process, but it isn't the specific property this question is highlighting - it's testing the entropy change from the ordered solid breaking apart into dispersed ions, not the overall free energy condition. B: This has it backwards - dissolving breaks apart the ordered solid lattice into freely moving, dispersed ions, which is an increase in disorder, not a decrease. C: Dissolving NaOH is actually exothermic - it releases heat and noticeably warms the solution, unlike substances such as ammonium nitrate that cool the solution down when they dissolve. Assuming all dissolution is cooling is the trap here.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0548$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Atomic Structure & Bonding$q$, $q$Ionic vs Covalent Character — Fajan's Rules$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Bonding in metal halides.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "epcv95", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Metallic bonding describes the 'sea of electrons' holding metal atoms together in a pure metal lattice - it doesn't apply to a compound formed between a metal and a nonmetal like bromine.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Dative (coordinate) bonding involves one atom donating both electrons of a shared pair to a bond - that's not the mechanism at play in a simple metal-halide compound like this.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "While some metal halides do show covalent character, the standard reasoning taught for a metal with clearly variable ionic charges reacting with an electronegative nonmetal is straightforward electron transfer - ionic bonding, not sharing.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A metal X forms two bromides with the formulae XBr2 and XBr3. What type of bonding exists between X and bromine in the bromides?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Ionic bonding", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "Metallic bonding", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Dative bonding", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Covalent bonding", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$The clue here is the variable charge - X shows up as both 2+ and 3+, which is exactly the kind of behaviour you see in a metal that loses different numbers of electrons depending on the reaction. That electron loss is the signature of ionic bonding: X transfers electrons to the highly electronegative bromine atoms, forming X2+ or X3+ cations alongside Br- anions.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Metallic bonding describes the 'sea of electrons' holding metal atoms together in a pure metal lattice - it doesn't apply to a compound formed between a metal and a nonmetal like bromine. C: Dative (coordinate) bonding involves one atom donating both electrons of a shared pair to a bond - that's not the mechanism at play in a simple metal-halide compound like this. D: While some metal halides do show covalent character, the standard reasoning taught for a metal with clearly variable ionic charges reacting with an electronegative nonmetal is straightforward electron transfer - ionic bonding, not sharing.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0549$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Separation of Mixtures$q$, $q$Separation Techniques$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Separating immiscible liquids.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kc1jua", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Centrifugation is for separating suspended solid particles from a liquid (or spinning down different-density solids), not for splitting two liquid layers that have already settled naturally by density.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Distillation separates miscible liquids based on differing boiling points - but petrol and water don't mix in the first place, so there's no need for a heat-based separation when a simple funnel does the job.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Filter paper separates solid particles from a liquid - there's no solid here, just two liquids, so filtration doesn't apply.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A mixture of petrol and water can be separated through$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "centrifugation", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "distillation", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "the use of a separating funnel", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "the use of filter paper", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Petrol and water don't mix - they're immiscible liquids that naturally settle into two distinct layers based on density, with petrol floating on top of water. That's exactly the situation a separating funnel is designed for: you simply let the layers settle and run off the bottom layer through the tap, leaving the top layer behind.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Centrifugation is for separating suspended solid particles from a liquid (or spinning down different-density solids), not for splitting two liquid layers that have already settled naturally by density. B: Distillation separates miscible liquids based on differing boiling points - but petrol and water don't mix in the first place, so there's no need for a heat-based separation when a simple funnel does the job. D: Filter paper separates solid particles from a liquid - there's no solid here, just two liquids, so filtration doesn't apply.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0550$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Nucleophilic substitution reactions$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Nucleophilic substitution reactions.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "nuclq7", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Hydrophobic describes how a substance interacts with water (water-repelling) - it has nothing to do with cyanide's role as an electron donor in this substitution mechanism.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Hydrophilic describes attraction to water - again, a solubility property, not a description of cyanide's electron-donating behaviour in this reaction.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This is the exact opposite classification - an electrophile seeks electrons, while cyanide is electron-rich and donates them, making it the nucleophile, not the electrophile, in this reaction.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$CH3CH2Cl + KCN → CH3CH2CN + KCl. In the reaction above, the cyanide is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "hydrophobic", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "hydrophilic", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "nucleophilic", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "electrophilic", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Look at what's actually happening in this reaction: the cyanide ion (CN-) is electron-rich, and it attacks the carbon atom that's bonded to the electronegative, electron-poor chlorine. That's a species donating a pair of electrons to form a new bond - the defining behaviour of a nucleophile ('nucleus-loving'). This is a classic SN2 substitution, and cyanide's role as the attacking nucleophile is exactly what makes it work.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Hydrophobic describes how a substance interacts with water (water-repelling) - it has nothing to do with cyanide's role as an electron donor in this substitution mechanism. B: Hydrophilic describes attraction to water - again, a solubility property, not a description of cyanide's electron-donating behaviour in this reaction. D: This is the exact opposite classification - an electrophile seeks electrons, while cyanide is electron-rich and donates them, making it the nucleophile, not the electrophile, in this reaction.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0551$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Benzene and Aromatic Substitution$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Industrial synthesis of styrene.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "uej13e", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$recall$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Ethyne (acetylene) has its own industrial uses, like producing vinyl chloride or as an oxy-acetylene fuel, but it's not the hydrocarbon used in the standard route to styrene.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Propyne isn't involved in styrene synthesis at all - it doesn't fit into the standard benzene-alkylation route used industrially.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Ethane is saturated and unreactive, lacking the double bond needed to alkylate benzene - it's ethene's reactive double bond that makes the styrene synthesis route possible, not ethane.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The hydrocarbon used in the production of styrene is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "ethyne", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "propyne", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "ethane", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "ethene", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Styrene production starts by reacting benzene with ethene in a Friedel-Crafts alkylation to form ethylbenzene, which is then dehydrogenated to give styrene. Ethene's reactive carbon-carbon double bond is exactly what makes it suitable for that alkylation step - the saturated, unreactive ethane can't do this job.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Ethyne (acetylene) has its own industrial uses, like producing vinyl chloride or as an oxy-acetylene fuel, but it's not the hydrocarbon used in the standard route to styrene. B: Propyne isn't involved in styrene synthesis at all - it doesn't fit into the standard benzene-alkylation route used industrially. C: Ethane is saturated and unreactive, lacking the double bond needed to alkylate benzene - it's ethene's reactive double bond that makes the styrene synthesis route possible, not ethane.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0552$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Isomerism$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Structural isomerism in haloalkanes.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "j32bof", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "This undercounts by one - it's easy to find three of the four isomers and miss the branched tertiary chloride (2-chloro-2-methylpropane) if you don't systematically check both the straight and branched skeletons.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This only counts the two positions on the straight-chain skeleton, forgetting that butane's branched isomer (2-methylpropane) also exists and offers two more chlorine positions of its own.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This assumes there's only one possible carbon skeleton and one place to put the chlorine - missing both the branching possibility and the multiple substitution positions available.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$How many structural isomers of chlorobutane are possible?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "4", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Don't just think about moving the chlorine along one carbon chain - remember butane itself has two skeletal forms: straight-chain and branched. On the straight chain, chlorine can sit on carbon 1 or carbon 2 (giving 1-chlorobutane and 2-chlorobutane). On the branched skeleton (2-methylpropane), chlorine can sit on a terminal carbon or on the central carbon (giving 1-chloro-2-methylpropane and 2-chloro-2-methylpropane). That's four genuinely distinct structures in total.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: This undercounts by one - it's easy to find three of the four isomers and miss the branched tertiary chloride (2-chloro-2-methylpropane) if you don't systematically check both the straight and branched skeletons. C: This only counts the two positions on the straight-chain skeleton, forgetting that butane's branched isomer (2-methylpropane) also exists and offers two more chlorine positions of its own. D: This assumes there's only one possible carbon skeleton and one place to put the chlorine - missing both the branching possibility and the multiple substitution positions available.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0553$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemistry & Industry$q$, $q$Petroleum Refining$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Catalytic reforming of hydrocarbons.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "yxuq7s", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$recall$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Cracking breaks large hydrocarbon molecules down into smaller ones - it's about shortening chains, not reshaping a chain's carbon skeleton into a ring.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Isomerization rearranges a straight-chain alkane into a branched-chain alkane with the same molecular formula - it changes branching, not ring formation.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Polymerization joins many small monomer molecules together into one large chain - an entirely different process from reshaping a single alkane molecule into a ring.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The conversion of open chain alkanes into cycloalkanes and aromatic compounds is called$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "cracking", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "reforming", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "isomerization", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "polymerization", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Reforming is the specific petroleum-refining process that reshapes straight-chain alkane molecules into rings - cycloalkanes and aromatic compounds - using heat and a catalyst. It's done industrially to boost the octane rating of petrol, since ring-shaped and aromatic molecules burn more smoothly in engines than their straight-chain counterparts.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Cracking breaks large hydrocarbon molecules down into smaller ones - it's about shortening chains, not reshaping a chain's carbon skeleton into a ring. C: Isomerization rearranges a straight-chain alkane into a branched-chain alkane with the same molecular formula - it changes branching, not ring formation. D: Polymerization joins many small monomer molecules together into one large chain - an entirely different process from reshaping a single alkane molecule into a ring.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0554$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Atomic Structure & Bonding$q$, $q$Intermolecular Forces — Van der Waals$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Boiling points of alkanes and van der Waals forces.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "zxcqb1", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$application$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Ethane's short two-carbon chain gives it very weak van der Waals forces, so it actually has one of the lowest boiling points of the group, not the highest.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Propane is a bit larger than ethane, but it's still much shorter than pentane, so its intermolecular forces - and its boiling point - are correspondingly lower.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Methane is the smallest alkane of all, with the weakest intermolecular forces in the group, giving it the lowest boiling point rather than the highest.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following alkanes has the highest boiling point?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Pentane", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ethane", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Propane", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Methane", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Among alkanes, boiling point climbs steadily as the carbon chain gets longer. Longer chains have more surface area in contact with neighbouring molecules, which means stronger van der Waals forces holding the molecules together - and stronger intermolecular forces mean you need more energy (a higher temperature) to boil the liquid. Of the four given, pentane (C5H12) has the longest chain, so it has the highest boiling point.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Ethane's short two-carbon chain gives it very weak van der Waals forces, so it actually has one of the lowest boiling points of the group, not the highest. C: Propane is a bit larger than ethane, but it's still much shorter than pentane, so its intermolecular forces - and its boiling point - are correspondingly lower. D: Methane is the smallest alkane of all, with the weakest intermolecular forces in the group, giving it the lowest boiling point rather than the highest.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0555$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Functional Group Identification — Esters$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Composition of natural waxes.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kmmznm", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Amino acids are the building blocks of proteins - they have no structural role in the composition of waxes.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Glycerol is specifically the alcohol component used to build fats and oils (triglycerides) - waxes instead use a single long-chain alcohol, not glycerol.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Alkanols (long-chain alcohols) are only one of the two ingredients that react to form a wax - the wax itself is the resulting ester (the alkanoate), not the free alcohol alone.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Certain useful waxes are composed mainly of$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "amino acids", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "glycerols", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "alkanols", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "alkanoates", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Waxes are esters - formed when a long-chain alkanoic acid reacts with a long-chain alkanol (a single monohydric alcohol, unlike the glycerol used in fats). The resulting ester compound is named as an 'alkanoate', and that ester itself is what makes up the wax, not the free alcohol or acid on its own.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Amino acids are the building blocks of proteins - they have no structural role in the composition of waxes. B: Glycerol is specifically the alcohol component used to build fats and oils (triglycerides) - waxes instead use a single long-chain alcohol, not glycerol. C: Alkanols (long-chain alcohols) are only one of the two ingredients that react to form a wax - the wax itself is the resulting ester (the alkanoate), not the free alcohol alone.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0556$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Air and Water$q$, $q$Water Treatment and Hardness$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Temporary hardness and limescale formation.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "beypy2", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Calcium tetraoxosulphate(VI) (calcium sulphate) is actually the compound responsible for permanent hardness, which isn't removed by boiling - it doesn't decompose on heating the way the temporary-hardness compound does.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This is the soluble compound present in the water before boiling - it's the cause of the hardness, not the insoluble deposit that forms afterward once boiling drives off the water and CO2.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Aluminium plays no role in water hardness chemistry - the relevant metal here is calcium, not aluminium.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$When temporary hard water is boiled for some time in a kettle, the inner surface of the kettle becomes coated with a deposit of$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "calcium tetraoxosulphate(VI)", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "calcium trioxocarbonate(IV)", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "calcium hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV)", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "aluminium trioxocarbonate(IV)", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Temporary hardness comes from dissolved calcium hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV), Ca(HCO3)2, which is soluble and stays invisible in the water. Boiling drives off the dissolved CO2 and water, and the reaction Ca(HCO3)2 → CaCO3 + H2O + CO2 converts that soluble compound into insoluble calcium trioxocarbonate(IV) - which is exactly the chalky 'fur' or limescale you see building up inside a kettle over time.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Calcium tetraoxosulphate(VI) (calcium sulphate) is actually the compound responsible for permanent hardness, which isn't removed by boiling - it doesn't decompose on heating the way the temporary-hardness compound does. C: This is the soluble compound present in the water before boiling - it's the cause of the hardness, not the insoluble deposit that forms afterward once boiling drives off the water and CO2. D: Aluminium plays no role in water hardness chemistry - the relevant metal here is calcium, not aluminium.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0557$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Solubility$q$, $q$Solubility and Saturation$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Saturated vs supersaturated solutions.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "mu3oy8", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Adding 'in the presence of undissolved solute' contradicts what makes a solution supersaturated - all the excess solute must be dissolved, with no visible solid present, for it to count as supersaturated rather than just an ordinary saturated solution with leftover solid.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This actually describes an ordinary saturated solution - dissolved to capacity, with extra undissolved solid visible at the bottom - not a supersaturated one, which holds more than that capacity.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This is simply the definition of a saturated solution - exactly at capacity, no more, no less - missing the 'more than it can normally dissolve' element that defines supersaturation specifically.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A supersaturated solution is said to contain$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "more solute than it can dissolve at that temperature in the presence of undissolved solute", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "as much solute as it can dissolve at that temperature in the presence of undissolved solute", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "as much solute as it can dissolve at that temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "more solute than it can dissolve at that temperature", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$The key idea with supersaturation is that ALL of the excess solute is still dissolved - there's no visible undissolved solid sitting at the bottom. It's an unstable, 'overloaded' state where the solution holds more solute than it normally could at that temperature, often achieved by dissolving solute at a higher temperature and then carefully cooling without disturbance.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Adding 'in the presence of undissolved solute' contradicts what makes a solution supersaturated - all the excess solute must be dissolved, with no visible solid present, for it to count as supersaturated rather than just an ordinary saturated solution with leftover solid. B: This actually describes an ordinary saturated solution - dissolved to capacity, with extra undissolved solid visible at the bottom - not a supersaturated one, which holds more than that capacity. C: This is simply the definition of a saturated solution - exactly at capacity, no more, no less - missing the 'more than it can normally dissolve' element that defines supersaturation specifically.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0558$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Air and Water$q$, $q$null$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Role of nitrogen in air.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "oin8lb", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "While diluted oxygen technically slows any oxidation process, the standard pairing taught for nitrogen's role is respiration and combustion - swapping in corrosion here misses the intended answer.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Same issue in the other direction - combustion, not corrosion, is the second process paired with respiration in the standard explanation of nitrogen's role in air.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Transpiration is water loss from plant leaves - a process unrelated to oxygen concentration or the chemistry nitrogen dilution affects.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The presence of nitrogen in air is to slow down$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "respiration and combustion", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "combustion and corrosion", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "corrosion and respiration", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "respiration and transpiration", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Nitrogen makes up about 78% of air and is largely chemically inert - its main practical role is to dilute the oxygen. If air were pure oxygen instead of a nitrogen-oxygen mix, both combustion and respiration would happen far too violently and rapidly to be safe or sustainable. Nitrogen's dilution effect moderates the pace of these two everyday oxygen-driven processes to something manageable.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: While diluted oxygen technically slows any oxidation process, the standard pairing taught for nitrogen's role is respiration and combustion - swapping in corrosion here misses the intended answer. C: Same issue in the other direction - combustion, not corrosion, is the second process paired with respiration in the standard explanation of nitrogen's role in air. D: Transpiration is water loss from plant leaves - a process unrelated to oxygen concentration or the chemistry nitrogen dilution affects.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0559$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Nature of Matter$q$, $q$Deliquescent and Hygroscopic Substances$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Hygroscopic, deliquescent and efflorescent substances.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "syb7rg", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Deliquescent substances do absorb moisture, but they absorb so much that they dissolve entirely into a liquid solution - the more general and standard answer for what makes a good drying agent is simply 'hygroscopic'.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Effervescence describes fizzing or bubbling as gas is released - it has nothing to do with a substance's ability to absorb moisture.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This is the opposite property - efflorescent substances lose their own water of crystallization to the air, which means they'd add moisture to the surroundings rather than remove it, making them a poor drying agent.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A good drying agent should be$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "deliquescent", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "effervescent", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "hygroscopic", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "efflorescent", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$A drying agent's whole job is to pull moisture out of its surroundings - that general affinity for absorbing water vapour from the air or a gas stream is exactly what 'hygroscopic' means, and it's the property examiners are testing for here. Deliquescence is a more extreme, related idea (absorbing so much water the solid dissolves into a puddle), but 'hygroscopic' is the standard umbrella term used to describe the desired property of a desiccant.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Deliquescent substances do absorb moisture, but they absorb so much that they dissolve entirely into a liquid solution - the more general and standard answer for what makes a good drying agent is simply 'hygroscopic'. B: Effervescence describes fizzing or bubbling as gas is released - it has nothing to do with a substance's ability to absorb moisture. D: This is the opposite property - efflorescent substances lose their own water of crystallization to the air, which means they'd add moisture to the surroundings rather than remove it, making them a poor drying agent.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0560$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Environmental Pollution$q$, $q$Pollution Control$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Causes of smog.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "alfbvy", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Fewer plants means fewer pollutants get absorbed, which can make smog worse once it forms - but the primary trigger is still the burning of fossil fuels releasing the pollutants in the first place.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Being near water isn't a direct cause of smog formation - it's an unrelated geographical feature.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Overpopulation on its own doesn't create smog - it's specifically the fossil fuel burning often associated with dense, industrial cities that generates the pollutants, not population density by itself.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Smog usually occurs over cities which$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "burn a lot of fossil fuels", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "have very few plants", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "are surrounded by water", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "are overpopulated", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Smog - a blend of 'smoke' and 'fog' - forms when pollutants from burning fossil fuels (vehicle exhaust, industrial emissions) build up in the air and react with atmospheric moisture and sunlight. The direct root cause the question is testing is that fossil fuel combustion, not any secondary factor about the city's geography or population alone.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Fewer plants means fewer pollutants get absorbed, which can make smog worse once it forms - but the primary trigger is still the burning of fossil fuels releasing the pollutants in the first place. C: Being near water isn't a direct cause of smog formation - it's an unrelated geographical feature. D: Overpopulation on its own doesn't create smog - it's specifically the fossil fuel burning often associated with dense, industrial cities that generates the pollutants, not population density by itself.$q$);
